@node Account and program paths
@section Account and program paths

@vindex sgn-account
@vindex sgn-cli-program
@vindex sgn-data-directory
@vindex sgn-db-directory
The user option @code{sgn-account} specifies the registered Signal phone number@comma{} for example @samp{+15550000000}.  This must match the account linked to @samp{signal-cli}.  The value is required: @code{sgn-start} will signal an error if it is @code{nil}.  The default is @code{nil}. @*

The user option @code{sgn-cli-program} specifies the path to the @samp{signal-cli} executable.  The default is the result of @code{(executable-find "signal-cli")}@comma{} or the bare name @samp{"signal-cli"} if it is not found when @samp{sgn} is loaded.  If @samp{signal-cli} is on your @code{$PATH}@comma{} the default should work without modification. @*

The user option @code{sgn-data-directory} specifies the directory where @samp{signal-cli} stores its data@comma{} including attachments and sticker packs.  @samp{sgn} reads attachment and sticker files from it but does not pass it to @samp{signal-cli}@comma{} so it must match where @samp{signal-cli} actually keeps its data.  The default is @samp{signal-cli} under @samp{$XDG_DATA_HOME} if that environment variable is set@comma{} and @samp{~/.local/share/signal-cli} otherwise@comma{} which is also where @samp{signal-cli} looks by default.  You would change this if you run @samp{signal-cli} with a non-standard data path. @*

The user option @code{sgn-db-directory} specifies the directory where @samp{sgn} stores its SQLite database@comma{} @samp{sgn.db}.  The default is @samp{sgn} under @samp{$XDG_DATA_HOME} if set@comma{} and @samp{~/.local/share/sgn} otherwise.  The directory is created automatically on first run. @*

@node Message display
@section Message display

@vindex sgn-history-page-size
@vindex sgn-message-grouping-interval
@vindex sgn-timestamp-format
@vindex sgn-prompt
The user option @code{sgn-history-page-size} controls how many of a chat's latest messages are shown when its buffer is first opened@comma{} and how many older ones each @code{sgn-load-more-history} adds (@ref{Reading a chat, , Reading a chat}).  The default is 50.  Increase this if you want to see more history immediately; decrease it for faster redrawing of busy chats@comma{} since the whole visible history is redrawn whenever the chat changes. @*

The user option @code{sgn-message-grouping-interval} specifies the number of seconds within which consecutive messages from the same sender are grouped under a single header.  The default is 300 (five minutes).  Set this to 0 to disable grouping entirely@comma{} or increase it to group messages over longer spans. @*

The user option @code{sgn-timestamp-format} controls how timestamps in message headers are displayed.  It accepts two values: @code{smart} (the default) shows the time for today's messages@comma{} the weekday and time for this week's@comma{} and the date and time for older ones; @code{absolute} always shows the date and time. @*

The user option @code{sgn-prompt} specifies the input prompt string displayed at the bottom of chat buffers.  The default is @samp{"> "}.  The history is always drawn above the prompt. @*

@node Media rendering
@section Media rendering

@vindex sgn-image-max-width
@vindex sgn-sticker-max-width
@vindex sgn-enable-animation
@vindex sgn-audio-players
The user option @code{sgn-image-max-width} sets the maximum pixel width for inline image rendering in chat buffers.  The default is 300.  Increase this on high-resolution displays or if you want larger image previews. @*

The user option @code{sgn-sticker-max-width} sets the maximum pixel width for sticker images.  The default is 150.  Stickers are typically smaller than photos@comma{} so a separate limit keeps them from appearing disproportionately large. @*

The user option @code{sgn-enable-animation} controls whether animated images@comma{} such as animated stickers and GIFs@comma{} are animated.  The default is @code{t}.  Set this to @code{nil} if animations are distracting or cause performance issues. @*

The user option @code{sgn-audio-players} lists the programs tried@comma{} in order@comma{} to play voice notes.  Each entry is a list of a program name followed by the arguments to put before the file name.  The first program found on @code{exec-path} is used.  The default tries @samp{mpv --no-video --really-quiet}@comma{} then @samp{ffplay -nodisp -autoexit -loglevel quiet}@comma{} then @samp{afplay} (available on macOS).  Signal voice notes are AAC audio@comma{} so the player must be able to decode it; add an entry if you prefer another player. @*

@node Messaging behavior
@section Messaging behavior

@vindex sgn-auto-open-buffer
@vindex sgn-send-read-receipts
@vindex sgn-send-typing
@vindex sgn-send-timeout
The user option @code{sgn-auto-open-buffer} controls whether a chat buffer is automatically displayed when an incoming message arrives in a chat you are not looking at.  The default is @code{nil}@comma{} meaning new messages only update the unread count and trigger notifications.  Set this to @code{t} if you want Emacs to display the relevant chat buffer on each incoming message. @*

The user option @code{sgn-send-read-receipts} controls whether @samp{sgn} sends read receipts via @code{sendReceipt} for messages you read in Emacs.  The default is @code{t}.  Reading is @emph{focus-gated}: a chat's messages count as read only when its buffer is shown in the selected window of a focused frame.  That happens when you switch to the chat by any means@comma{} when Emacs regains focus@comma{} or when a message arrives while you are looking at it; @code{sgn-dashboard-mark-read} also marks a chat read (@ref{Dashboard, , Dashboard}).  With this option set to @code{nil}@comma{} messages are still marked read in @samp{sgn}@comma{} but no receipts are sent.  @samp{signal-cli} also syncs the receipts to your other linked devices@comma{} so messages you read in Emacs show as read on your phone.  Unread state is kept per message@comma{} and messages you read on another device are marked read in @samp{sgn} when that device's read sync arrives.  Reactions@comma{} edits@comma{} and deletions do not count as unread messages. @*

The user option @code{sgn-send-typing} controls whether typing indicators are sent to your chat partner while you compose a message.  The default is @code{t}.  A start indicator is sent when you begin typing and repeated every ten seconds while you keep typing; a stop indicator is sent after five seconds without typing@comma{} when you clear the input@comma{} or when you send the message.  No indicators are sent in Note to Self. @*

The user option @code{sgn-send-timeout} specifies how many seconds to wait for @samp{signal-cli} to confirm a sent message.  The default is 30.  A message still unconfirmed after this long is labelled @emph{not confirmed: may not have been sent} in the chat buffer; the label disappears if confirmation arrives later.  Increase it if you are on a slow connection and often see the label on messages that were in fact delivered. @*

@node Linking
@section Linking

@vindex sgn-link-device-name
@vindex sgn-link-qrencode-program
The user option @code{sgn-link-device-name} specifies the device name that @code{sgn-link} registers@comma{} as shown in the phone's list of linked devices.  The default is @samp{"Emacs (sgn)"}. @*

The user option @code{sgn-link-qrencode-program} specifies the @samp{qrencode} executable used to draw the linking QR code.  Without it@comma{} @code{sgn-link} shows the linking URI as text only.  The default is @samp{"qrencode"}. @*

@node Notifications
@section Notifications

@vindex sgn-notification-style
@vindex sgn-desktop-notifications
The user option @code{sgn-notification-style} determines where the global unread count indicator is displayed.  It accepts two values: @code{modeline} (the default) adds a @samp{[sgn:N]} indicator to the global mode line; @code{tab-bar} adds a segment to the tab bar instead.  The indicator is managed by the minor mode @code{sgn-global-mode} (@ref{Global indicator, , Global indicator}). @*

The user option @code{sgn-desktop-notifications} controls whether desktop notifications are shown for incoming messages.  The default is @code{t}.  Notifications are only shown for chats you are not looking at@comma{} and never for muted chats.  Messages arriving in the same chat within about a second are announced together: a single message shows its sender and a preview@comma{} with spoilers concealed@comma{} and several show a count such as ``3 new messages''.  A message you read before the notification is due is not announced.  In groups@comma{} the title names both the sender and the group.  On macOS@comma{} notifications use AppleScript (@samp{osascript}); on GNU/Linux@comma{} they use D-Bus via @code{notifications-notify}@comma{} and activating the notification opens the chat.  On other systems the notification is shown in the echo area. @*

