@node Starting and stopping
@section Starting and stopping

@findex sgn-start
@findex sgn-stop
@findex sgn-show-log
The command @code{sgn-start} initializes the @samp{sgn} Signal client.  It checks that @code{sgn-account} is set and that no linking is in progress@comma{} opens (and if needed creates or migrates) the database@comma{} loads the account's ACI and the contact name cache@comma{} and marks messages left @emph{sending} by an earlier session as not confirmed@comma{} since their replies can no longer arrive.  It then starts the JSON-RPC subprocess@comma{} refreshes contacts and groups now and every five minutes@comma{} starts a timer that deletes expired disappearing messages every ten seconds@comma{} enables the global unread indicator@comma{} and refreshes the dashboard.  If @samp{sgn} is already running@comma{} it only says so.  Most commands that need @samp{signal-cli} start @samp{sgn} themselves@comma{} so you rarely need to call this directly. @*

The command @code{sgn-stop} shuts down the client.  It saves the drafts of all chat buffers@comma{} cancels the expiry and contact refresh timers@comma{} stops the JSON-RPC subprocess@comma{} closes the database@comma{} and disables the global indicator.  Requests still waiting for a reply are abandoned: a message being sent is labelled @emph{not confirmed: may not have been sent}. @*

If @samp{signal-cli} exits abnormally@comma{} @samp{sgn} does not restart it.  The global indicator changes to @samp{[sgn:offline]}@comma{} the dashboard shows the reason above the chat list@comma{} and commands that need @samp{signal-cli} signal the same error instead of starting it again.  The reason includes the last line @samp{signal-cli} printed to its standard error@comma{} which is kept in the hidden buffer @samp{" *sgn-stderr*"}.  The message names the command that fixes the problem: @code{sgn-link} when the device is no longer linked (@ref{Linking this device, , Linking this device})@comma{} @code{sgn-start} otherwise.  Running @code{sgn-start} clears the failure. @*

The command @code{sgn-show-log} displays the @samp{*sgn-log*} debug buffer.  This buffer records all JSON-RPC traffic and internal events@comma{} which is useful for troubleshooting connectivity or message delivery issues.  When it grows beyond about four million characters@comma{} its oldest half is dropped. @*

@node Linking this device
@section Linking this device

@findex sgn-link
@samp{signal-cli} runs as a device linked to your phone's Signal account.  Signal removes linked devices that have been offline for a long time.  If @samp{sgn} has not run for about a month@comma{} @samp{signal-cli} then fails with ``User @dots{} is not registered''@comma{} and no new messages arrive.  Running @samp{sgn} regularly keeps the link alive. @*

The command @code{sgn-link} links @samp{signal-cli} again.  It first stops @samp{sgn}@comma{} since @samp{signal-cli} locks the account while linking; @code{sgn-start} refuses to run until linking is over.  It then runs @samp{signal-cli link} and shows a QR code in the @samp{*sgn link*} buffer.  Scan it from the phone under Signal > Settings > Linked devices > Link new device; the code expires after about two minutes@comma{} after which @code{sgn-link} can be run again.  Killing the @samp{*sgn link*} buffer cancels linking.  If linking fails@comma{} the buffer shows everything @samp{signal-cli} printed@comma{} and @samp{sgn} stays stopped.  Once linking succeeds@comma{} the command starts @samp{sgn} and warns if the linked number differs from @code{sgn-account}.  If a Signal Desktop database and @samp{sqlcipher} are available@comma{} it then offers to run @code{sgn-import-from-desktop} (@ref{Importing from Signal Desktop, , Importing from Signal Desktop})@comma{} since Signal does not deliver the messages sent while a device was unlinked.  The import skips messages already in the database. @*

@node Opening chats
@section Opening chats

@findex sgn-chat
@findex sgn-note-to-self
@findex sgn-dashboard
The command @code{sgn-chat} prompts for a chat using @code{completing-read} and opens it@comma{} starting @samp{sgn} first if needed.  The candidates are all chats@comma{} most recent first@comma{} followed by contacts you have no chat with yet.  Each candidate carries a hidden stable chat ID@comma{} so selection works correctly even when several contacts share a display name; such names are disambiguated with the contact's phone number or the start of their ID@.  Annotations show unread counts and relative timestamps.  You can also type a phone number with a leading @samp{+} that is not among the candidates. @*

The command @code{sgn-note-to-self} opens the ``Note to Self'' chat@comma{} which is the conversation with your own Signal account. @*

The command @code{sgn-dashboard} opens the chat list buffer (@ref{Dashboard, , Dashboard}).  If the @samp{sgn} service is not running@comma{} it starts it first@comma{} unless @samp{signal-cli} has already failed@comma{} in which case the dashboard shows the failure. @*

@node Reading a chat
@section Reading a chat

@findex sgn-chat-mode
@findex sgn-chat-return
@findex sgn-open-at-point
@findex sgn-load-more-history
A chat buffer@comma{} in @code{sgn-chat-mode}@comma{} has two parts: the message history@comma{} which is read-only@comma{} and below it the prompt and the input area.  Each chat has at most one buffer@comma{} named @samp{*sgn: <display-name>*}; the buffer is found by its chat ID@comma{} not its name@comma{} and is renamed when the contact's or group's name changes.  The header line shows the chat's name@comma{} its disappearing message timer (for example @samp{⏱ 1d}) if one is set@comma{} and who is typing. @*

The history is always drawn from the database@comma{} in timestamp order@comma{} and is redrawn whenever the chat changes@comma{} keeping the input@comma{} its undo history@comma{} point@comma{} and each window's position.  Messages are grouped under a header naming the sender and time (@ref{Message display, , Message display}).  Each message may show@comma{} besides its text@comma{} a quote of the message it replies to@comma{} its media@comma{} its reactions grouped by emoji@comma{} a @samp{📌} mark if pinned@comma{} and annotations: @samp{(edited)}@comma{} the send status label of a message you sent (@ref{Sending messages and media, , Sending messages and media})@comma{} and on your own sent messages a @samp{✓} once a recipient's device received it and @samp{✓✓} once a recipient read or viewed it.  Mentions are shown as @samp{@@Name}.  A deleted message is replaced by @samp{[Message deleted]}.  An incoming poll is shown as text beginning with @samp{📊} and the question; @samp{sgn} cannot vote in or create polls. @*

The command @code{sgn-chat-return}@comma{} bound to @samp{RET}@comma{} sends the input when point is in the input area and otherwise calls @code{sgn-open-at-point}. @*

The command @code{sgn-open-at-point} acts on what is at point in the history.  On a concealed spoiler@comma{} it reveals the spoilers of that message until the buffer is killed.  On a voice note@comma{} it plays it with the first program found in @code{sgn-audio-players}; pressing @samp{RET} again while it plays stops it.  On an image or file@comma{} it opens the file with the system's default application.  On a URL@comma{} it opens the URL with @code{browse-url}.  Media that @samp{signal-cli} has not downloaded is shown as @samp{[Media not downloaded]} and cannot be opened. @*

The command @code{sgn-load-more-history} shows @code{sgn-history-page-size} more of the chat's older messages@comma{} or reports that all history is loaded.  It is bound to @samp{g} on messages and to @samp{C-c C-l} anywhere in the chat buffer. @*

@node Sending messages and media
@section Sending messages and media

@findex sgn-chat-send-input
@findex sgn-attach-file
The command @code{sgn-chat-send-input} sends the text in the input area to the current chat.  If the @samp{sgn} service is not already running@comma{} sending starts it before issuing the RPC request.  If a reply is in progress@comma{} the message is sent as a quoted reply; if an edit is in progress@comma{} the message replaces the original.  Lightweight markup in the input is parsed into Signal style ranges before sending (@ref{Text formatting, , Text formatting}).  The message appears in the chat at once.  It is labelled @emph{sending…} until @samp{signal-cli} confirms it@comma{} after which the label disappears and the message takes the timestamp Signal assigned.  If @samp{signal-cli} rejects the message@comma{} it is labelled @emph{not sent} and the reason is shown in the echo area; if some group members did not receive it@comma{} @emph{not delivered to every recipient}; and if no confirmation arrives within @code{sgn-send-timeout} seconds@comma{} or @samp{signal-cli} stops first@comma{} @emph{not confirmed: may not have been sent}.  Requests are never retried automatically@comma{} since a send whose reply was lost may still have been delivered.  A message sent in a chat with a disappearing timer starts its countdown when it is sent.  The input is saved as a draft two seconds after you stop typing and restored when the chat buffer is opened again@comma{} including after restarting Emacs.  It is sent by @samp{RET} in the input area; use @samp{S-<return>} or @samp{C-j} to insert a newline. @*

The command @code{sgn-attach-file} sends a file as an attachment to the current chat.  It prompts for an existing file and an optional caption@comma{} which is sent as the message text.  The attachment appears in the chat at once@comma{} with the same send status labels as a text message.  Bound to @samp{C-c C-a}. @*

@node Message actions
@section Message actions

@findex sgn-react
@findex sgn-reply
@findex sgn-edit
@findex sgn-delete
@findex sgn-forward
@findex sgn-copy-text
@findex sgn-toggle-pin
@findex sgn-chat-cancel-action
These commands operate on the message at point in a chat buffer.  Point can be anywhere within a message's text property region.  Their single-key bindings live in @code{sgn-chat-message-map} (@ref{Keymaps, , Keymaps})@comma{} so they are active only when point is on a message. @*

Other clients refer to a message by the timestamp Signal assigned to it@comma{} so actions that target a message (reacting@comma{} replying@comma{} editing@comma{} deleting for everyone@comma{} pinning) refuse a message of yours that is still @emph{sending…} or was not confirmed as sent.  They also refuse deleted messages. @*

The command @code{sgn-react} sends a reaction to the message at point.  It prompts with @code{completing-read} over common emoji@comma{} shown with their names (for example ``👍 thumbs up''); you can also type any emoji directly.  If you have already reacted to the message@comma{} invoking @code{sgn-react} again removes your reaction instead of prompting.  The reaction is shown at once and removed again@comma{} with the reason in the echo area@comma{} if @samp{signal-cli} rejects it.  Bound to @samp{R}.  Reactions from contacts@comma{} and those you send from your other linked devices@comma{} appear under the message as they arrive. @*

The command @code{sgn-reply} sets up a quoted reply to the message at point.  The prompt changes to show the quoted context@comma{} and the next message you send quotes it.  Press @samp{C-g} (@code{sgn-chat-cancel-action}) to cancel.  Bound to @samp{r}. @*

The command @code{sgn-edit} places the text of your own message at point into the input area for editing.  The text is pre-filled with markup reconstructed from the message's styles@comma{} so @samp{*bold*} text comes back as @samp{*bold*}@comma{} and characters that would otherwise be read as markup are escaped with a backslash.  Only your own messages with text can be edited.  When you send@comma{} the new text is shown at once with the label @emph{sending…} and sent with @code{editTimestamp} so Signal replaces the original; if @samp{signal-cli} rejects the edit@comma{} the previous text is restored and the reason shown.  Press @samp{C-g} to cancel.  Bound to @samp{e}. @*

The command @code{sgn-delete} deletes your own message at point for everyone@comma{} after confirmation with @code{y-or-n-p}.  The message is replaced by @samp{[Message deleted]} once @samp{signal-cli} confirms the deletion; if it fails@comma{} the reason is shown and the message is kept.  With a prefix argument@comma{} or for a message that was not sent or not confirmed@comma{} it deletes the message from @samp{sgn} only@comma{} without notifying anyone; this works on any message@comma{} not just your own.  Deleting removes the message's text and attachment files.  Bound to @samp{d}. @*

The command @code{sgn-forward} sends the message at point@comma{} with its text and attachments@comma{} to another chat selected via @code{completing-read}.  The text keeps its formatting@comma{} unless it contains mentions: these are forwarded as plain @samp{@@Name} text@comma{} and the formatting is then dropped.  The forwarded copy is stored in the target chat and shows its own send status there.  Bound to @samp{f}. @*

The command @code{sgn-copy-text} copies the message text at point to the kill ring@comma{} with mentions written as @samp{@@Name}.  Bound to @samp{c}. @*

The command @code{sgn-toggle-pin} pins or unpins the message at point.  If the message is already pinned@comma{} it is unpinned; otherwise@comma{} it is pinned.  The change is applied in @samp{sgn} once @samp{signal-cli} confirms it.  Pinned messages display a @samp{📌} mark in the chat buffer; pins and unpins made by others or on your other devices are shown as they arrive.  Bound to @samp{P}. @*

The command @code{sgn-chat-cancel-action} cancels an in-progress reply or edit@comma{} restoring the normal prompt; cancelling an edit also clears the input.  If no action is in progress@comma{} it falls through to @code{keyboard-quit}.  Bound to @samp{C-g}. @*

Changes made by others arrive the same way.  Incoming edits replace a message's text and mark it @samp{(edited)}; a remote delete is honoured only when it comes from the message's sender@comma{} including deletions you make from your phone; reactions@comma{} pins@comma{} delivery and read receipts@comma{} and read syncs from your other devices update the chat as they arrive.  An edit@comma{} deletion or read sync that arrives before the message it refers to is kept and applied when that message arrives.  A message @samp{signal-cli} could not decrypt@comma{} for instance because the sender's safety number changed@comma{} appears as a warning in the sender's chat instead of being dropped. @*

@node Searching
@section Searching

@findex sgn-search
@findex sgn-search-in-chat
@findex sgn-search-refresh
@findex sgn-search-goto-result
@findex sgn-search-next-result
@findex sgn-search-prev-result
@findex sgn-search-mode
The command @code{sgn-search} performs a full-text search across all conversations using FTS5.  It prompts for a query and shows up to 100 matching messages@comma{} newest first@comma{} in the @samp{*sgn Search*} buffer@comma{} in @code{sgn-search-mode}.  The words of the query are matched literally: punctuation and FTS5 operators such as @samp{OR} or @samp{*} have no special meaning@comma{} and a message matches only if it contains every word.  Each result shows the sender@comma{} the chat@comma{} the time@comma{} and the message text with the matches in brackets.  Deleted messages are excluded. @*

The command @code{sgn-search-in-chat} restricts the search to the current chat.  Bound to @samp{C-c C-s} in chat buffers. @*

The command @code{sgn-search-refresh} re-runs the most recent search@comma{} in the same scope: a search in one chat stays in that chat.  Bound to @samp{g} in the results buffer. @*

In the search results buffer@comma{} @code{sgn-search-goto-result} (bound to @samp{RET}) opens the chat containing the result at point and moves point to the message itself@comma{} loading older history if needed.  @code{sgn-search-next-result} (@samp{n}) and @code{sgn-search-prev-result} (@samp{p}) navigate between results@comma{} and @samp{s} starts a new search across all chats. @*

@node Dashboard
@section Dashboard

@findex sgn-dashboard-mode
@findex sgn-dashboard-refresh
@findex sgn-dashboard-open
@findex sgn-dashboard-mark-read
@findex sgn-dashboard-toggle-mute
@findex sgn-dashboard-toggle-pin
The dashboard is a @code{tabulated-list-mode} buffer named @samp{*sgn*}@comma{} in @code{sgn-dashboard-mode}@comma{} that displays all conversations with messages@comma{} sorted by last message timestamp@comma{} with pinned chats always at the top.  Each row shows the chat name (marked @samp{📌} if pinned)@comma{} a preview of the last message with its sender@comma{} a relative timestamp@comma{} and an unread count.  Spoilers are concealed in previews; a muted chat shows @samp{🔇}@comma{} and a group whose name is unknown shows as ``Unnamed group''.  Chats with unread messages are displayed in bold.  Long text is truncated with a fade-out gradient effect instead of an ellipsis.  The columns fill the window width: the chat name takes about a third of the space@comma{} between 20 and 40 columns@comma{} and the preview takes the rest.  They are resized whenever the window is. @*

The command @code{sgn-dashboard-open} opens the chat at point.  Bound to @samp{RET}. @*

The command @code{sgn-dashboard-mark-read} marks the unread messages of the chat at point as read and@comma{} if @code{sgn-send-read-receipts} is non-nil@comma{} sends read receipts for them.  Bound to @samp{d}. @*

The command @code{sgn-dashboard-toggle-mute} mutes or unmutes the chat at point.  Muted chats are excluded from the global unread count and suppress desktop notifications.  The setting is local to @samp{sgn}.  Bound to @samp{M}. @*

The command @code{sgn-dashboard-toggle-pin} pins or unpins the chat at point.  Pinned chats are sorted to the top of the list.  This chat pin is local to @samp{sgn} and unrelated to pinning a message.  Bound to @samp{P}. @*

The command @code{sgn-dashboard-refresh} reloads the dashboard data from the database.  Bound to @samp{g}.  The dashboard also refreshes automatically whenever a chat changes. @*

The dashboard also binds @samp{c} to @code{sgn-chat} and @samp{s} to @code{sgn-search}. @*

@node Group and contact management
@section Group and contact management

@findex sgn-create-group
@findex sgn-set-disappearing
@findex sgn-block-contact
@findex sgn-unblock-contact
@findex sgn-contacts-refresh
The command @code{sgn-create-group} creates a new Signal group.  It prompts for a group name and then repeatedly prompts for members with @code{completing-read} until you enter an empty string.  Once @samp{signal-cli} confirms@comma{} the new group's chat is opened; if it fails@comma{} the reason is shown. @*

The command @code{sgn-set-disappearing} sets the disappearing message timer for the current chat.  It prompts for a duration in seconds; entering 0 disables the timer.  The command dispatches to @code{updateGroup} for groups and @code{updateContact} for individual chats@comma{} and records the new timer once @samp{signal-cli} confirms it; if it fails@comma{} the reason is shown.  Timer changes made by others@comma{} and the timer carried by each incoming message@comma{} are recorded too.  An incoming message with a timer starts counting down when you read it@comma{} and a message you send starts when it is sent; expired messages are deleted@comma{} attachments included@comma{} by a timer that runs every ten seconds while @samp{sgn} is running. @*

The command @code{sgn-block-contact} blocks a contact or group after confirmation.  The command @code{sgn-unblock-contact} unblocks a previously blocked one.  Both prompt via @code{completing-read} and report whether @samp{signal-cli} succeeded. @*

The command @code{sgn-contacts-refresh} fetches the latest contacts and groups from @samp{signal-cli} and updates both the in-memory cache and the database.  A contact's display name is their nickname@comma{} the name you gave them@comma{} their profile name@comma{} or their username@comma{} in that order of preference.  When a contact entry or a group's member list pairs a phone number with an ACI@comma{} data stored under the number is merged into the ACI@.  This runs automatically on startup and every five minutes@comma{} but you can invoke it manually if a contact name has not yet updated. @*

@node Global indicator
@section Global indicator

@findex sgn-global-mode
The minor mode @code{sgn-global-mode} activates the global unread count indicator.  When enabled@comma{} it displays the number of unread messages in chats that are not muted@comma{} in either the mode line or tab bar@comma{} depending on the value of @code{sgn-notification-style} (@ref{Notifications, , Notifications}).  Nothing is displayed when there are no unread messages.  If @samp{signal-cli} has failed@comma{} it displays @samp{sgn:offline} instead; hovering over it shows the reason and the command that fixes it.  This mode is enabled automatically by @code{sgn-start} and disabled by @code{sgn-stop}. @*

@node Text formatting
@section Text formatting

Messages you compose may contain lightweight markup: @samp{*bold*}@comma{} @samp{_italic_}@comma{} @samp{~strikethrough~}@comma{} @samp{`monospace`} and @samp{||spoiler||}.  The markup is removed from the text and sent as Signal style ranges.  An opening delimiter must start a word and be followed by a non-blank character; a closing one must follow a non-blank character and end a word.  Thus @samp{snake_case_name} and @samp{2*3*4} are left alone.  URLs are never parsed for markup@comma{} and text inside a monospace span is not parsed for other markup.  A delimiter preceded by a backslash is taken literally and the backslash is dropped@comma{} so @samp{\*not bold\*} is sent as @samp{*not bold*}. @*

Incoming styles are rendered with the faces listed in @ref{Text formatting faces, , Text formatting faces}.  Spoilers are concealed until you press @samp{RET} on them (@code{sgn-open-at-point}@comma{} @ref{Reading a chat, , Reading a chat}).  Style and mention ranges are stored in UTF-16 code units@comma{} as Signal sends them@comma{} so formatting lines up correctly in text containing emoji. @*

@node Importing from Signal Desktop
@section Importing from Signal Desktop

@findex sgn-import-from-desktop
The command @code{sgn-import-from-desktop} imports text message history from Signal Desktop's SQLCipher database into @samp{sgn}'s SQLite database.  This requires the @samp{sqlcipher} CLI tool (installable via @samp{brew install sqlcipher} on macOS) and a local Signal Desktop installation; the command looks for Signal Desktop's data under @samp{~/Library/Application Support/Signal}@comma{} its location on macOS@.  It starts @samp{sgn} first if needed. @*

The import exports the relevant tables of Signal Desktop's encrypted database to a temporary plain SQLite file@comma{} which is deleted afterwards.  Only incoming and outgoing messages with text are imported@comma{} together with their quotes and their reactions@comma{} taken from each message's own record; attachments@comma{} stories@comma{} and messages that were deleted for everyone are not.  Messages whose disappearing timer has already run out are skipped@comma{} and the others keep counting down.  Imported messages are marked read.  Chat names from Signal Desktop are used only for chats @samp{sgn} has no name for.  The whole import runs in one database transaction@comma{} and messages already in the database are skipped@comma{} so the command can safely be run again to fill a gap in the history@comma{} for example after relinking with @code{sgn-link}.  When it finishes@comma{} it reports the numbers of new messages and of reactions imported. @*

The database key is read from Signal Desktop's @samp{config.json}.  Both the legacy plain-text key and the newer Chromium safeStorage encrypted key are handled; the latter is decrypted with the password stored in the macOS Keychain@comma{} using @samp{node}@comma{} so this format is only supported on macOS@.  The key and the SQL that uses it are passed to the helper programs on their standard input@comma{} never on a command line where other processes could read them. @*

