@findex sgn-db-get-recipients
The function @code{sgn-db-ensure-chat} creates a chat of type @samp{"individual"} or @samp{"group"} unless it exists@comma{} and @code{sgn-db-update-chat} sets columns of a chat given as a plist of keywords such as @code{:name}@comma{} @code{:muted}@comma{} @code{:pinned} or @code{:expiration}.  @code{sgn-db-get-chat} returns a chat as a plist@comma{} including its computed @code{:unread} count; @code{sgn-db-get-chats} returns all chats with messages (or all chats@comma{} with its optional argument)@comma{} pinned first and then most recent first; @code{sgn-db-chat-type} returns a chat's type.  @code{sgn-db-total-unread} returns the number of unread messages in chats that are not muted. @*

The function @code{sgn-db-insert-message} inserts a message given as a plist of columns and returns its rowid@comma{} or @code{nil} if a message with the same chat@comma{} sender and timestamp is already stored.  @code{sgn-db-update-message} sets columns of a message by rowid.  @code{sgn-db-get-message} finds a message by chat@comma{} sender and Signal timestamp; @code{sgn-db-get-message-by-rowid} by rowid; and @code{sgn-db-get-messages} returns a chat's latest messages (50 by default)@comma{} oldest first@comma{} leaving out expired ones. @*

The function @code{sgn-db-unread-messages} returns the sender@comma{} timestamp and rowid of each unread incoming message in a chat.  @code{sgn-db-mark-read} marks a message read at a given time@comma{} unless it already is@comma{} and starts its disappearing timer if it has one.  @code{sgn-db-mark-deleted} drops a message's content and media but keeps the row as a tombstone@comma{} while @code{sgn-db-delete-message} removes the message with its reactions and pins.  Both return the message's media@comma{} whose files the caller may delete with @code{sgn-media-delete-files}. @*

The remaining accessors serve the modules above.  @code{sgn-db-touch-chat} records a timestamp as a chat's latest activity if it is newer@comma{} and @code{sgn-db-latest-messages} returns a hash table from each chat ID to its latest message@comma{} for the dashboard.  @code{sgn-db-count-messages} counts a chat's stored messages@comma{} and @code{sgn-db-find-own-message} finds one of your messages by its Signal timestamp@comma{} for receipts.  @code{sgn-db-settle-interrupted-sends}@comma{} called by @code{sgn-start}@comma{} relabels sends and edits still marked as in progress as not confirmed.  @code{sgn-db-expired-messages} lists messages whose timer ran out before a given time.  @code{sgn-db-insert-media} and @code{sgn-db-get-media} store and return a message's media plists.  @code{sgn-db-save-draft} and @code{sgn-db-get-draft} store and return a chat's draft@comma{} a blank draft clearing it.  @code{sgn-db-get-meta} and @code{sgn-db-set-meta} read and write the key-value @samp{meta} table@comma{} which holds the account's ACI@comma{} and @code{sgn-db-get-recipients} returns every known person's ACI@comma{} number and name. @*

@node Reactions receipts and pins
@subsection Reactions@comma{} receipts and pins

@findex sgn-db-set-reaction
@findex sgn-db-get-reactions
@findex sgn-db-add-receipt
@findex sgn-db-receipt-types
@findex sgn-db-set-pin
@findex sgn-db-pinned-p
Reactions@comma{} receipts and pins refer to their message by its author and Signal timestamp rather than by rowid@comma{} so they can be stored before the message arrives.  The function @code{sgn-db-set-reaction} records a sender's reaction to a message@comma{} or its removal when the emoji is @code{nil}; an update older than the stored one is ignored.  @code{sgn-db-get-reactions} returns the current reactions to a message.  @code{sgn-db-add-receipt} records a delivery@comma{} read or viewed receipt for one of your messages@comma{} and @code{sgn-db-receipt-types} returns the receipt types recorded for it@comma{} from which the @samp{✓} and @samp{✓✓} marks are drawn.  @code{sgn-db-set-pin} records a pin@comma{} or an unpin when the pinner is @code{nil}@comma{} and @code{sgn-db-pinned-p} tells whether a message is pinned. @*

@node Identity merging
@subsection Identity merging

@findex sgn-db-merge-identity
@findex sgn-db-identity-needs-merge-p
@findex sgn-db-upsert-recipient
@findex sgn-db-uuid-for-number
The function @code{sgn-db-merge-identity} rewrites every reference to a phone number as the given ACI@comma{} in one transaction: the chat stored under the number is merged into the chat under the ACI (keeping the latest activity@comma{} any mute or pin@comma{} the draft and the name)@comma{} and message senders@comma{} quote authors@comma{} reactions@comma{} pins@comma{} receipts and pending mutations are rewritten.  A message stored under both identities is merged into one copy@comma{} which keeps its media@comma{} or takes the other copy's@comma{} and counts as read if either was.  It returns non-nil if anything changed.  It is called whenever the contact list or an envelope pairs a number with an ACI@comma{} and runs @code{sgn-db-merge-functions} after a merge; @code{sgn-db-identity-needs-merge-p} tells whether any stored data still refers to a number. @*

The function @code{sgn-db-upsert-recipient} records an ACI's phone number and display name@comma{} leaving stored values unchanged when an argument is @code{nil}.  @code{sgn-db-uuid-for-number} returns the ACI recorded for a phone number. @*

@node Pending mutations and search
@subsection Pending mutations and search

@findex sgn-db-add-pending
@findex sgn-db-take-pending
@findex sgn-db-purge-pending
@findex sgn-db-search
The function @code{sgn-db-add-pending} keeps an edit@comma{} delete or read mutation for a message not stored yet@comma{} and @code{sgn-db-take-pending} removes and returns the mutations for a message@comma{} oldest first@comma{} when it arrives.  Read syncs do not say which chat a message belongs to@comma{} so they are recorded under the chat ID @samp{"*"} and match the message in any chat.  @code{sgn-db-purge-pending} forgets mutations recorded before a given time. @*

The function @code{sgn-db-search} performs a full-text search using FTS5.  It accepts a QUERY string@comma{} an optional CHAT-ID to restrict the search@comma{} and an optional LIMIT (default 50).  Each word of the query is quoted@comma{} so it is matched literally@comma{} and a message must contain every word.  Results are message plists@comma{} newest first@comma{} with a @code{:snippet} key holding the message text with matches delimited by brackets.  Deleted messages are excluded. @*

@node RPC layer
@section RPC layer

@findex sgn-rpc-start
@findex sgn-rpc-stop
@findex sgn-rpc-alive-p
@findex sgn-rpc-send
@findex sgn-rpc-failure-advice
@findex sgn-rpc-unlinked-p
@findex sgn-rpc-parse-json
The function @code{sgn-rpc-start} starts the @samp{signal-cli} JSON-RPC subprocess@comma{} as @samp{signal-cli -a ACCOUNT jsonRpc}@comma{} stopping any previous one and clearing the recorded failure.  The process talks over a pipe rather than a pty: on macOS a pty drops input beyond 1024 bytes per line@comma{} which would silently lose long requests such as replies quoting long messages.  Its standard error goes to the hidden buffer @samp{" *sgn-stderr*"}@comma{} which is emptied at each start. @*

The function @code{sgn-rpc-stop} stops the subprocess.  Every request still waiting for a reply is abandoned: its error callback is called with an error object whose @code{abandoned} entry is non-nil@comma{} since the request may or may not have taken effect.  The same happens when the process exits on its own.  The function @code{sgn-rpc-alive-p} returns non-nil if the subprocess is running. @*

The function @code{sgn-rpc-send} sends a JSON-RPC request.  It accepts a METHOD name@comma{} a PARAMS alist@comma{} and optional CALLBACK and ERROR-CALLBACK functions@comma{} and returns the request ID@.  The callback is called with the result when a successful reply arrives.  The error callback is called with the error object when @samp{signal-cli} reports an error or stops before replying; without one@comma{} the error is logged and shown in the echo area.  Requests are never retried@comma{} because a send whose reply was lost may still have been delivered@comma{} and sending it again could duplicate it.  If @samp{signal-cli} is not running@comma{} @code{sgn-rpc-send} signals a @code{user-error} naming the failure@comma{} if any@comma{} and the command that fixes it; it does not start @samp{sgn} itself (see @code{sgn-ensure-running}@comma{} @ref{Session and read state, , Session and read state}). @*

The function @code{sgn-rpc-failure-advice} returns the description of the last abnormal exit of @samp{signal-cli}@comma{} followed by the command that fixes it@comma{} or @code{nil} if there was none.  @code{sgn-rpc-unlinked-p} returns non-nil if that failure means this device is no longer linked.  @code{sgn-rpc-parse-json} parses JSON the way @samp{sgn} reads @samp{signal-cli} output: objects become alists@comma{} arrays lists@comma{} and @samp{null} and @samp{false} become @code{nil}. @*

